{
  "image_name": "3c2949a729aa4f643dee7af3611930f5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how radio-frequency switching can be integrated into a compact wireless transceiver and what the switch must do electrically. On the left, RF devices are shown stacked directly above standard silicon CMOS circuits, emphasizing 3D monolithic integration: the RF layer sits on top of the digital/baseband silicon so the two can be connected vertically within one chip.\n\nThe center schematic places the RF switch in the analogue RF front-end between the antenna and the rest of the transceiver. The switch routes signals to different frequency bands and to transmit or receive paths that include band switching, duplexers and filters, a power amplifier for transmit, and a low-noise amplifier for receive. These analogue blocks interface with the digital baseband processor through DAC and ADC blocks, indicating the overall signal flow from digital processing to wireless transmission and back.\n\nThe right side summarizes the key operating states of the RF switch. In the ON state, it behaves like a small resistance, so the important metric is insertion loss, meaning how much desired signal power is lost while passing through. In the OFF state, it behaves like a small capacitance, and the key metric is isolation, meaning how well unwanted signal leakage is suppressed.",
